{"subjectId":"python","id":"collections-more","title":"More collections: namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap","group":"Data Structures","order":22,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The rest of the <code>collections</code> toolbox — lightweight records, counting arithmetic, order-aware dicts, layered lookups — plus when to use each.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tag\"></i>namedtuple — a tuple with field names</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import namedtuple\n\nPoint = namedtuple(\"Point\", [\"x\", \"y\"])\np = Point(3, 4)\nx, y = p                             # still a tuple: unpacking, indexing, hashing\nprint(p.x, p[1], x + y)              # 3 4 7\nprint(p._replace(x=10), p)           # Point(x=10, y=4) Point(x=3, y=4)\nprint(p._asdict())                   # {'x': 3, 'y': 4}\nprint(Point._fields)                 # ('x', 'y')\nprint(Point._make([5, 6]))           # Point(x=5, y=6)\n\nConn = namedtuple(\"Conn\", \"host port timeout\", defaults=[30])\nprint(Conn(\"db\", 5432))              # Conn(host='db', port=5432, timeout=30)</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>typing.NamedTuple</code> is the class-syntax version — same runtime behaviour, but with type hints, docstrings and methods.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from typing import NamedTuple\n\nclass Employee(NamedTuple):\n    name: str\n    dept: str = \"eng\"\n\n    def badge(self) -&gt; str:\n        return f\"{self.name}@{self.dept}\"\n\ne = Employee(\"asha\")\nprint(e, e.badge())          # Employee(name='asha', dept='eng') asha@eng\ntry:\n    e.name = \"ravi\"\nexcept AttributeError:\n    print(\"immutable\")       # immutable</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>namedtuple</code> / <code>NamedTuple</code></th><th><code>@dataclass</code></th><th><code>dict</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mutable</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (or <code>frozen=True</code>)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Access</td><td><code>p.x</code> and <code>p[0]</code></td><td><code>p.x</code></td><td><code>p['x']</code></td></tr><tr><td>Hashable</td><td>Yes</td><td>Only if frozen</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Small immutable records, returning several values</td><td>Rich objects with defaults &amp; methods</td><td>Dynamic / JSON-like data</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Counter — counting with arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import Counter\n\ninventory = Counter(apple=3, pear=1)\ninventory.update([\"apple\", \"kiwi\"])          # add counts\nprint(inventory)                             # Counter({'apple': 4, 'pear': 1, 'kiwi': 1})\ninventory.subtract({\"pear\": 2})              # can go negative\nprint(inventory[\"pear\"], inventory.total())  # -1 4\n\na, b = Counter(\"aabbc\"), Counter(\"abd\")\nprint(a + b)          # Counter({'a': 3, 'b': 3, 'c': 1, 'd': 1})\nprint(a - b)          # Counter({'a': 1, 'b': 1, 'c': 1})   ← drops counts &lt;= 0\nprint(a &amp; b)          # Counter({'a': 1, 'b': 1})           ← min of each\nprint(sorted(Counter(x=2, y=1).elements()))   # ['x', 'x', 'y']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A <code>Counter</code> is a <code>dict</code> subclass: missing keys read as <code>0</code>, and <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>&amp;</code>, <code>|</code> combine counters. <code>most_common(k)</code> gives the top k.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-folders\"></i>defaultdict variations</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import defaultdict\n\nseen_first = defaultdict(lambda: \"unknown\")     # any zero-arg callable\nprint(seen_first[\"x\"])                          # unknown\n\ndef tree():\n    return defaultdict(tree)                    # infinitely nested dict\nt = tree()\nt[\"india\"][\"ts\"][\"hyd\"] = 1\nprint(t[\"india\"][\"ts\"][\"hyd\"], \"ap\" in t[\"india\"])   # 1 False\n\ntotals = defaultdict(int)\nfor dept, amount in [(\"eng\", 5), (\"ops\", 2), (\"eng\", 3)]:\n    totals[dept] += amount\nprint(dict(totals))                              # {'eng': 8, 'ops': 2}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Merely <em>reading</em> <code>d[missing]</code> on a <code>defaultdict</code> inserts the key. Use <code>key in d</code> or <code>d.get(key)</code> when you only want to check.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>OrderedDict — order you can manipulate</h3>\n<p>Plain dicts keep insertion order since 3.7, so <code>OrderedDict</code> is now for its extras: <code>move_to_end</code>, <code>popitem(last=False)</code>, and order-sensitive equality.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import OrderedDict\n\na = OrderedDict(x=1, y=2)\nb = OrderedDict(y=2, x=1)\nprint(a == b, dict(a) == dict(b))     # False True   ← OrderedDict compares order\n\na.move_to_end(\"x\")                    # x becomes the newest\nprint(list(a))                        # ['y', 'x']\nprint(a.popitem(last=False))          # ('y', 2)   ← FIFO pop of the oldest</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python — LRU cache (a classic interview question)</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.data = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.data:\n            return -1\n        self.data.move_to_end(key)            # mark as recently used\n        return self.data[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        self.data[key] = value\n        self.data.move_to_end(key)\n        if len(self.data) &gt; self.capacity:\n            self.data.popitem(last=False)     # evict least recently used\n\ncache = LRUCache(2)\ncache.put(1, \"a\"); cache.put(2, \"b\")\ncache.get(1)                                  # 1 is now most recent\ncache.put(3, \"c\")                             # evicts 2\nprint(list(cache.data), cache.get(2))         # [1, 3] -1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>ChainMap — layered lookups</h3>\n<p><code>ChainMap(a, b, c)</code> searches several dicts in order <strong>without copying</strong> them. Writes go to the first map. Perfect for defaults → config file → environment → command-line layering, or nested scopes.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import ChainMap\n\ndefaults = {\"theme\": \"light\", \"lang\": \"en\", \"debug\": False}\nuser = {\"theme\": \"dark\"}\ncli = {\"debug\": True}\n\nsettings = ChainMap(cli, user, defaults)\nprint(settings[\"theme\"], settings[\"lang\"], settings[\"debug\"])   # dark en True\n\nsettings[\"lang\"] = \"te\"                  # written to the first map (cli)\nprint(cli, defaults[\"lang\"])             # {'debug': True, 'lang': 'te'} en\n\nscope = ChainMap({\"x\": 1})\ninner = scope.new_child({\"x\": 2})        # push a new scope\nprint(inner[\"x\"], inner.parents[\"x\"])    # 2 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-user-cog\"></i>UserDict &amp; UserList — safe subclassing</h3>\n<p>Subclassing <code>dict</code> directly is tricky: <code>update()</code> and the constructor bypass your overridden <code>__setitem__</code>. <code>UserDict</code> stores the data in <code>self.data</code> and routes every write through your methods.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import UserDict\n\nclass LowerDict(dict):\n    def __setitem__(self, k, v):\n        super().__setitem__(k.lower(), v)\n\nclass LowerUserDict(UserDict):\n    def __setitem__(self, k, v):\n        super().__setitem__(k.lower(), v)\n\nprint(LowerDict({\"A\": 1}))                    # {'A': 1}   ← override bypassed!\nprint(LowerUserDict({\"A\": 1}).data)           # {'a': 1}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i>Two more handy containers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from array import array\nfrom types import SimpleNamespace\n\nnums = array(\"i\", [1, 2, 3])        # compact typed array of C ints\nnums.append(4)\nprint(nums.tolist(), nums.itemsize) # [1, 2, 3, 4] 4\n\ncfg = SimpleNamespace(host=\"localhost\", port=8000)   # attribute bag\ncfg.debug = True\nprint(cfg.port, cfg)   # 8000 namespace(host='localhost', port=8000, debug=True)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checklist\"></i>Choosing the right container</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Immutable record with named fields</td><td><code>namedtuple</code> / <code>typing.NamedTuple</code></td></tr><tr><td>Count things, combine counts</td><td><code>Counter</code></td></tr><tr><td>Group values / auto-create missing entries</td><td><code>defaultdict</code></td></tr><tr><td>Reorder keys, LRU cache, FIFO dict</td><td><code>OrderedDict</code></td></tr><tr><td>Look up through several dicts in priority order</td><td><code>ChainMap</code></td></tr><tr><td>Queue / sliding window</td><td><code>deque</code></td></tr><tr><td>Custom dict/list behaviour</td><td><code>UserDict</code> / <code>UserList</code></td></tr><tr><td>Millions of numbers, low memory</td><td><code>array</code> (or NumPy)</td></tr><tr><td>Quick attribute bag</td><td><code>types.SimpleNamespace</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why use OrderedDict now that dict preserves insertion order?</summary><div><p>For its extra behaviour: <code>move_to_end</code>, <code>popitem(last=False)</code> (FIFO) and equality that takes order into account. It's the idiomatic base for an LRU cache.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Implement an LRU cache in O(1) per operation.</summary><div><p>Use an <code>OrderedDict</code>: on <code>get</code>, <code>move_to_end(key)</code>; on <code>put</code>, insert, <code>move_to_end</code>, and if over capacity <code>popitem(last=False)</code>. Under the hood this is a hash map plus a doubly linked list — the same design you'd write by hand in Java or C++. For caching function results, <code>functools.lru_cache</code> does it for you.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>namedtuple or dataclass?</summary><div><p><code>namedtuple</code> for small, immutable, tuple-compatible records (unpacking, hashing, low memory). <code>dataclass</code> when you need mutability, default factories, validation in <code>__post_init__</code> or many methods.</p></div></details></section>"}